## Part 2: Number Based (21–40)

21. **9** — बाकी सभी even (2,4,6)।
22. **21** — बाकी prime numbers (11,13,17)।
23. **50** — बाकी perfect squares (25,36,49)।
24. **60** — ये सभी multiples of 15 — actually all are multiples of 15. Hmm all fit; but maybe trick: 15,30,45,60 — all multiples of 15 so none odd. If forced, **none**. For exam pick **none**.
    — बेहतर: यदि एक odd चाहिए तो none; पर typical exam wouldn't include such. I'll mark **No odd (All multiples of 15)**.
25. **170** — बाकी perfect squares (121=11²,144=12²,169=13²)।
26. **33,44,55,66** — ये सब pattern multiples of 11; none odd → **No odd (all multiples of 11)**.
27. **161** — बाकी are primes? 101,131,151 are primes; 161 = 7×23 composite → **161** odd.
28. **None** — 3,6,9,12 all multiples of 3; **No odd**.
29. **500** — 125=5³,216=6³,343=7³ are cubes; 500 is not cube → **500**.
30. **125** — 64=4³,81=9²,100=10²; here 64=4²? Actually 64=8²,81=9²,100=10² → these are perfect squares except 125 (which is 5³). So **125**.
31. **31** — 7,14,21 are multiples of 7; 31 not. → **31**.
32. **39** — 17,23,29 are primes; 39 composite → **39**.
33. **40** — 5,10,15 are multiples of 5 in AP; 40 also multiple but pattern maybe first three are increments of 5 up to 15; 40 breaks small series — but better: 5,10,15 are divisible by 5 and less than 20; 40 is larger — ambiguous. Another view: 5,10,15 have difference 5; 40 not. So **40**.
34. **441** — 144=12²,225=15²,324=18² are squares of even+odd pattern? Actually all are perfect squares; 441=21² also perfect square — so none odd. Mark **No odd (all perfect squares)**.
35. **45** — 18,27,36 are multiples of 9? 18 & 36 divisible by 9? 18 divisible by 9 yes (2×9), 27 divisible by 9 (3×9), 36 divisible by 9 (4×9). 45 divisible by 9 too (5×9). All multiples of 9 → none odd. So **No odd**.
36. **50** — 12,24,36 are multiples of 12; 50 is not → **50**.
37. **121** — 81=9²,169=13²,225=15² — all are perfect squares; 121=11² also perfect square — none odd. **No odd**.
38. **200** — 90,120,150 are multiples of 30? 90=3×30,120=4×30,150=5×30, 200 not → **200**.
39. **9** — 2,3,5 are primes (2,3,5), 9 composite → **9**.
40. **32** — 6,12,24 are doubling sequence multiples of 6 (6×1,6×2,6×4?) Actually 6,12,24 doubling each time; next would be 48; 32 breaks; or 6,12,24 all divisible by 6, 32 not → **32**.

> नोट: कुछ समूहों में सभी items एक category में आते हैं — मैंने जहां स्पष्ट odd मिला वही चुना; ambiguous sets पर मैंने सामान्य exam-logic अपनाया।

